---
title: "Next season"
description: "The runbook for setting up a new competition: what to change, what recomputes, and what to check afterwards."
---

Running a new competition is mostly one file edit. This chapter is the checklist.

## Before you start

Decide two things with whoever is organising:

1. **The competition dates.** A Monday start and a Sunday end is the shape
   everything is tuned for. Other shapes work, but read the note at the end of this
   chapter first.
2. **Which guilds are competing, and how many members each actually has.**

The second is not a formality. The member count is the denominator of every
ranking in the competition.

## Step 1: the dates

`src/config.ts`:

```ts
export const COMPETITION_START = "2027-01-11";   // a Monday
export const COMPETITION_END   = "2027-03-07";   // the Sunday N weeks later, inclusive
```

Both are inclusive, both are `yyyy-mm-dd` in `Europe/Helsinki`.

::: {.callout-warning}
## A window that does not contain today makes everything silently do nothing

FR-26 excludes anything outside the window, so a window still in the future means
every tap lands on a refusal, the ticker returns immediately, and no reminder ever
fires. There is no error anywhere.

A test asserts that today falls inside the window. **If the suite is failing on
that test, the window has expired and this step is why you are here.**
:::

## Step 2: the guild roster

```ts
export const GUILDS: readonly Guild[] = [
  { slug: "tik", name: "TiK", memberCount: 700 },
  // ...
];
```

- **`slug`** is the key. It appears in deep links (`?startgroup=tik`), in callback
  payloads, and as a foreign key in `users` and `chats`. **Do not change an existing
  slug.**
- **`name`** is display only, and is overwritten in the database on every boot.
  Renaming is safe.
- **`memberCount`** is the denominator. Get this from each guild directly.

::: {.callout-important}
## The member counts in the repository are unverified

They were carried over from `SPEC.md` §1 as plausible figures, and `SPEC.md`
requires them to be re-verified with each guild before launch. That has not been
done.

A stale count has no symptom. There is no error and nothing looks wrong: one guild
is simply ranked incorrectly for the entire competition. This is the single
highest-value item in this runbook.
:::

**Adding a guild** is one entry plus a rebuild. **Removing one** is awkward:
`syncGuilds` only inserts and updates, so the `guilds` row stays, and it must,
because users and chats reference it. Dropping the entry from config stops the
picker offering it and makes its deep link answer with "that guild is gone",
which is the intended graceful degradation.

## Step 3: anything else worth revisiting

| Constant | Change it if |
|---|---|
| `TIER_MINUTES` | The organisers want different credit per tier. Changing it **recomputes all history**, so it is safe mid-competition, though it will visibly move the standings |
| `WEEKLY_TARGET_MINUTES` | Only with a reason. 150 is the WHO guideline and the copy assumes it |
| `MONDAY_POST_HOUR` | 9 is chosen because Monday morning is where student gym attendance measurably rises |
| `REMINDER_HOURS` in `src/strings.ts` | The four offered hours are 17, 18, 20, 21 |

## Step 4: rebuild

```sh
docker compose up -d --build
docker compose logs -f bot
```

**Not `restart`.** See [Running it](running.tmd).

On boot the bot migrates (a no-op if the schema is current) and runs `syncGuilds`,
which upserts every config entry into the `guilds` table. That is the moment the
new member counts take effect, retroactively.

## Step 5: verify

```sh
docker compose logs bot | tail -20        # expect "@<bot> polling"
bun run test:db                            # the window test should now pass
```

Then, in Telegram:

1. `/standings` in a private chat. The tables should render with the new guild
   list and the new per-member numbers.
2. `/log` and tap a tier. The confirmation should show a progress bar.
3. Check a bound group chat's pinned message updates within 15 minutes.

## Step 6: the smoke run

Work through `docs/SMOKE.md`. It is the only acceptance basis for the
Telegram-facing code, and **it has never been run.** Do not put a real competition
in front of several hundred people without it.

## Reusing the same database across seasons

Nothing in the schema is scoped to a season. If you point a new competition at a
database that already holds the previous one's data:

- **Standings are unaffected.** Both tables come from `standings(from, to)`, which
  bounds by date, and the season table's `from` is the new `COMPETITION_START`. Old
  rows fall outside it and are excluded.
- **Personal streaks are the exception**, and this is the one to watch.
  `weeklyTotals()` is **not** bounded by any date: it returns every week the user has
  a record in, for all time. `weeklyStreak` then walks backwards from the current week
  and stops at the first week below target, so an off-season gap breaks the chain in
  practice. Back-to-back seasons with no gap would carry a streak across the boundary.
- **Users stay registered**, which is usually what you want. Their `reminder_asked`
  flag also persists, so returning users are not asked again. If you want everyone
  re-asked, that is a deliberate `UPDATE users SET reminder_asked = FALSE`.
- **`ignored_streak` persists**, so anyone paused at the end of the last season
  starts the new one paused. Consider `UPDATE users SET ignored_streak = 0`.
- **`chats` bindings persist**, including `last_monday_week`. A binding from a
  previous season is stale but harmless: the first Monday of the new competition is
  later than the stored week, so the post fires normally.

Starting from an empty database avoids all four questions and is the simpler
choice if there is no reason to keep the history.

## If the competition does not end on a Sunday

This works, and one piece of code exists specifically to make it work, but know
what it does.

`isFinalMondayPost()` decides the closing post from `weekStart`, not from today's
date. With an end on a Wednesday, the Thursday after is already outside the
window while that same week's Monday post is still an ordinary one reporting the
week before. Reading today's date there would print "That's the competition" while
the competition was still running.

The pin refresh still stops on `COMPETITION_END` exactly, so the final pinned
standings freeze mid-week and stay frozen. That is intended.
